In Norfolk, your landlord has **45 days** to return your deposit. The clock starts when your lease ends or when you move out, whichever is later. Along with the money, they have to send an itemized statement of anything they kept.

**Before you move out:**
- **Ask to be at the move-out inspection.** You can request to attend. If you do, the inspection has to happen within 72 hours after you give the place back.
- **Find your move-in report.** Your landlord should have given you a written move-in damage report within 5 days of when you moved in. It's useful proof of damage that was already there.
- **If the landlord doesn't follow the rules:** If they wrongly keep your deposit on purpose, they can be made to return it and also pay your actual damages and attorney's fees.

**Source:** PropInspect's Virginia guide: https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/virginia (last reviewed September 18, 2026). It's based on Va. Code § 55.1-1226: https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1226/

This guide covers Virginia state law. It doesn't say anything specific to Norfolk, so check your lease for any extra terms. This is general information, not legal advice. If you end up in a dispute, talk to a Virginia landlord-tenant lawyer or a local tenant rights group.